<%# THE WHOLE CARD IS A LINK, NOT A DIV THAT NAVIGATES (design review B1).

    It was `<div onclick="window.location=…">`: no `href`, no tab stop, no focus ring, nothing for
    a screen reader to announce and no middle-click or ⌘-click to open in a new tab. The card was
    reachable by mouse alone, on the index that is the app's main way into a category.

    A BLOCK `<a>` ROUND THE CARD rather than a stretched-link pseudo-element, because there is
    nothing else clickable inside it — no nested link to be swallowed — so the simplest structure
    is also the correct one. The heading keeps its own text; the link's accessible name is the
    whole card, which reads as the category's name and its figures, and that is what a user
    arrowing through this grid wants to hear.

    `block` and `h-full` because an anchor is inline by default and the grid stretches its
    children: without both, the card collapses to the height of its text. The focus ring comes
    from custom.css's shared `:focus-visible` rule — no per-site treatment. %>
<a href="<%= category_path(category) %>" class="group block h-full rounded">
  <div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow duration-300 flex flex-col h-full">
    <div class="p-5 flex-grow">
      <div class="flex justify-between items-start mb-4">
        <div class="flex items-center">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded flex items-center justify-center" style="background-color: <%= category.display_color %>;">
            <% case category.category_type %>
            <% when "expense" %>
              <%= heroicon "arrow-trending-down", options: { class: "h-5 w-5 text-white" } %>
            <% when "income" %>
              <%= heroicon "arrow-trending-up", options: { class: "h-5 w-5 text-white" } %>
            <% end %>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-3">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900"><%= category.name %></h3>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center rounded px-2 py-1 text-xs font-medium ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-200 mt-1">
              <%= category.category_type.capitalize %>
            </span>
            <% unless category.tracked? %>
              <span class="inline-flex items-center rounded px-2 py-1 text-xs font-medium text-gray-400 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-200 mt-1 ml-1">
                Untracked
              </span>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    
    <div class="mt-2">
      <% calc = category.calculator(selected_date) %>
      <%# `expense?` rather than the deleted `pool_covered?`: the cap arm that used to follow this
          one is deleted with the cap it read (plan 3, task 3). %>
      <% if category.expense? %>
        <div class="mb-4">
          <div class="flex justify-between text-sm mb-1">
            <span class="text-gray-500">Spent <%= period_time_label %></span>
            <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= number_to_currency(calc.total_amount) %></span>
          </div>
          <%# ** WHAT THIS CATEGORY CLAIMS (computed-claims spec §2, §3.4). ** The line read
              "Holding" over a `HoldingCalculator#balance` — allocations in, less allocations out,
              less the spending attributed to the category. Nothing moves on the purpose side any
              more (§5), so there is no balance to read: a category's money is Σ its rules' claims,
              computed from the rules, the calendar and its spending at the instant it is asked for.
              `Claimed` is the noun the Budget page's group header already prints over the same sum.

              ** ONE PRESENTER PER CARD, AND ONE LEDGER FOR THE WHOLE PAGE. ** The figure and the
              bar's percentage are `CategoryBudgetPresenter`'s — the same object the show page's
              holdings card is built from — so the clamped percentage is spelled ONCE for the two
              screens that draw a goal bar. The old code had `HoldingCalculator#progress_percentage`
              shared for exactly that reason, and it is the reader whose comment carries the
              measurement: an overdrawn goal read `-30% complete` on this app's own demo data before
              the clamp existed, and a second copy of the arithmetic is how it would come back.

              `claim_ledger` IS THE INDEX'S ONE LEDGER — see `CategoriesController#claim_ledger`. It
              is built over the user's WHOLE rule set in three grouped statements, so this costs the
              same whether the page draws one card or thirty; a presenter left to build its own
              would be the thirtieth copy. Everything on it is lazy, so an index of income
              categories — none of which reaches this branch — runs no query for it at all.

              THE FUND TEST IS `Category#building_rule` (rules-own-the-budget spec §5), read through
              the presenter's `#bar?` — the same reader the show page's holdings card and the entry
              form's impact card ask, so a fund is a fund on every one of them. The BAR is the
              CAPPED fund's alone: a building rule with no target names no figure, and there is
              nothing for a track to be a fraction of. %>
          <% if category.holder? %>
            <% money = CategoryBudgetPresenter.new(category: category, claims: claim_ledger) %>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs mt-2" data-holding-line>
              <span class="text-gray-500">Claimed</span>
              <span class="font-medium text-gray-900">
                <%= number_to_currency(money.claim) %>
              </span>
            </div>
            <% if money.bar? %>
              <% progress = money.progress_percentage %>
              <div class="mt-2" data-building-progress>
                <div class="h-1.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
                  <div class="h-1.5 rounded-full <%= progress >= 100 ? "bg-status-success" : "bg-brand-dark" %>"
                       style="width: <%= progress %>%;"></div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">
                  <%= progress %>% of <%= number_to_currency(money.target) %>
                </p>
              </div>
            <% end %>
          <% else %>
            <%# NOT "comes out of what's available" — there is no `available` any more. `free` is a
                DEFINITION (`min(pot, total − Σ claims)`, §2) rather than a pot that spending is read
                against, and what is true of this category is that no rule speaks for its receipts,
                so they reduce `free` directly. Home's "This period" section says the same thing
                about the same population by simply printing what was spent, with no bar and no
                denominator, because there is nothing for a bar to be a fraction of. %>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2" data-holding-line>
              Nothing claims this — comes out of what's free to spend
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      <% elsif category.income? %>
        <div class="mb-4">
          <div class="flex justify-between text-sm mb-1">
            <span class="text-gray-500">Monthly Income</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= number_to_currency(calc.total_amount) %></span>
          </div>
          
          <div class="flex justify-between mt-2">
            <span class="text-xs text-gray-500">vs last month</span>
            <span class="text-xs flex items-center gap-1 <%= calc.previous_month_trend == :up ? "text-status-success" : "text-status-danger" %>">
              <%= heroicon calc.previous_month_trend == :up ? "arrow-up" : "arrow-down", options: { class: "h-3 w-3" } %>
              <%= calc.previous_month_change_percentage.abs %>%
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      <% end %>
      
      <% if category.items.any? %>
        <div class="mt-4">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 mb-2">Top Items</h4>
          <ul class="space-y-1">
            <% calc.top_items.each do |item, amount| %>
              <li class="flex justify-between text-sm">
                <span class="text-gray-600 truncate max-w-[70%]"><%= item.name %></span>
                <% case category.category_type %>
                <% when "expense" %>
                  <span class="text-status-danger font-medium">-<%= number_to_currency(amount) %></span>
                <% when "income" %>
                  <span class="text-status-success font-medium">+<%= number_to_currency(amount) %></span>
                <% end %>
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
        </div>
      <% else %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 italic">No items yet</p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
  
    <%# THE FOOTER IS ALWAYS THERE NOW (design review B1). It was `opacity-0
        group-hover:opacity-100` — an affordance that only appeared once you had already found the
        thing it describes, and never at all on a touch screen or to a keyboard user, while still
        occupying its strip of the card at every size. Permanently visible, and worded as what it
        is rather than as an instruction about a mouse. %>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 px-5 py-3 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div class="text-center">
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-600 group-hover:text-gray-900 transition-colors">View details</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</a>
